Japanese sample EPUBs for exercising the reader by hand on a device. Kept apart
from test/epubs so this fork's own material, with its own provenance, does not
mix into the upstream set. Nothing here is loaded by the gtest suites; copy a
file to an SD card and open it.

The four files
--------------

One book, built four ways:

                         full CSS (~144 KB)      minimal CSS (~2 KB)
  vertical, ppd=rtl      test_vertical_ja        test_vertical_ja_minicss
  horizontal, ppd=ltr    test_horizontal_ja      test_horizontal_ja_minicss

The chapter XHTML is byte-identical across a row, and across a column it differs
by one attribute -- class="vrtl" against class="hltr" on the body element. So the
set isolates two variables independently:

  along a row     writing mode, with the stylesheet held constant
  down a column   stylesheet size, with the writing mode held constant

That makes a difference attributable. A rendering fault that appears in the
minimal-CSS file too is in the layout engine; one that only appears with the full
stylesheet is in CSS handling. A fault present in both writing modes is not a
tategaki bug, whatever it looks like.

The horizontal pair also serves as the regression check for vertical work: the
vertical branch must not disturb it, and having the same text on both sides means
a regression shows up as a difference rather than as a hunch.

What the text covers
--------------------

12 chapters, 196 ruby instances, 41 of them read in Latin script. They include
annotations long enough to overhang their base word and to overhang the head and
foot of a column. Also list items, tilde variants (U+FF5E, U+301C, U+223C,
U+02DC), fullwidth and halfwidth digit runs of one, two and three digits,
fullwidth operators (＝ ＜ ＞ ≠ ≒), exclamation/question pairs, Greek letters,
and line-break positions where kinsoku rules apply. Most of the vertical fixes on
this branch were found with it.

The last chapter is about ruby alone, and each of its paragraphs is one question:
long marks and brackets inside a reading, readings that are a single Latin letter
or carry a hyphen, period, apostrophe or space, readings that are only digits
(where the body's own rule sets one and two upright and turns three), readings in
Cyrillic and Greek, readings that mix scripts, one-character bases carrying
readings far longer than themselves and set next to each other so they have to
collide, monoruby that fits its base exactly against monoruby that does not, and
a long passage seeded with annotated words so that some of them land on a column
break -- which they do not yet survive.

Provenance
----------

Text by OSAKANA TARO, published on Kakuyomu under the handle memoro and reused
here by its author. CSS derived from the EBPAJ EPUB 3 production guide template
set, ver. 1.1.4.

The EPUBs are built from that Kakuyomu source by NovelToEpub, a PowerShell script
at https://github.com/osakanataro/NovelToEpub, so they can be rebuilt rather than
hand-edited -- fix the text on Kakuyomu, re-run, replace the files.

Run the four from one working directory of your own, then copy the results here.
KAKUYOMU below stands for https://kakuyomu.jp/works/2912051602969738484.

  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -MaxCacheAgeHours 0.1 -OutputDir test_vertical_ja.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -MinimalCss -OutputDir test_vertical_ja_minicss.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -Horizontal -OutputDir test_horizontal_ja.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -Horizontal -MinimalCss -OutputDir test_horizontal_ja_minicss.epub

-OutputDir names the file when its value ends in .epub, and a directory otherwise.
Naming each one is what keeps the four apart: without it every run writes the
work's own title and the previous file is gone.

Run them from the same directory, and not from this one. The episode cache is a
.cache directory beside the output, so a shared working directory is what lets the
four share one fetch -- and pointing them here would leave that cache in the repo,
which .gitignore does not cover outside the root.

The script caches each episode it fetches, and -MaxCacheAgeHours defaults to 0,
which disables age-based expiry: a cached episode is reused however old it is.
Pass -MaxCacheAgeHours 0.1 on the *first* command only, as above, so it re-checks
Kakuyomu for revisions. The remaining three run within those six minutes and
reuse what the first fetched, so the site is polled once rather than four times.

Rebuild all four together. They are only worth what their shared text is worth,
and the source gains chapters over time -- regenerating one alone silently breaks
the comparison the set exists for.
